\title{Mnemonic principles for propositional logic laws}
\date{2026-01-31T19:09:46Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{There are two important organizing/mnemonic principles:
\ol{
\li{Most equivalences have an "opposite world" version where we
  switch #{\land / \lor} and #{\True / \False}.}
\li{We can get some intuition by thinking in terms of multiplication
  and addition:
  \ul{
  \li{#{\True} is kind of like 1}
  \li{#{\False} is kind of like 0}
  \li{#{\neg} is kind of like subtracting from 1}
  \li{#{\land} is kind of like #{\times}}
  \li{#{\lor} is kind of like #{+}, but with a maximum result of #{1}}
  }
  This analogy is not perfect, but it works as a mnemonic to help
  us remember some of the equivalences.
}}
}
